===Etymology===
* In 1923, the spelling "heeby jeebys" was used by U.S. cartoonist {{w|Billy De Beck}} in his comic strip ''{{w|Barney Google}}'' (''{{w|New York American}}'', 26 October 1923).
* In 1926, the spelling "heebie jeebies" was used by U.S. singer {{w|Louis Armstrong}} for his single {{w|Heebie Jeebies (composition)|"Heebie Jeebies"}} (written by {{w|Boyd Atkins}}).
* In 1928, the spelling "heebee-geebees" was used in the article "We All Have the Heebee-Geebees" by G. M. Keller of {{w|Swift & Company}} (''Modern School Store'', Vol. 11, p. <!--http://books.google.com/books?id=BfJIAAAAYAAJ&q=%22heebee-geebees%22#search_anchor -->269 via Google Books).
* In 1956, the spelling "heeby jeebies" was used by singer {{w|Little Richard}} on the cover of his homonymous SP record (and the hyphenated spelling "heeby-jeebies" on its vinyl disc itself).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhiː.biːˌdʒiː.biːz/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"hi:.bi:%dZi.bi:z/}}

===Noun===
{{en-plural noun}}

# {{context|US|idiomatic|lang=en}} A general feeling of [[anxiety]], [[fear]], [[uneasiness]], or [[nausea]].
#: ''I don't mind worms, but spiders give me the '''heebie-jeebies'''.''
#: ''When I see that weird house, I get the '''heebie-jeebies'''.''

====Usage notes====
Most commonly used with the verbs "give" and "get" (as in in the expressions "get the heebie-jeebies" and "give someone the heebie-jeebies").

